Two fixes needed in my githubSource heal:
1. `provider.ioCacheDrop('openPrs')` — the provider object in this tree: does `provider` (from smProvider.createSmProvider) expose ioCacheDrop? The provider stub in tests exposes ioCacheDrop(o,r,kind,id). In smProvider.js, ioCacheDrop is a MODULE-level function exported on the provider? Check line 965 — it's in module exports. Does the provider object returned by createSmProvider include ioCacheDrop? The test stub had `ioCacheDrop: function (o, r, kind, id)`. So call signature: (owner, repo, 'openPrs', null). There's also dropOpenPrsCache(repoInfo) helper in smAgent — but githubSource can't use smAgent's helper (wrong direction). In githubSource, smProviderModule is required at top (`require('../../common/smProvider.js')`) — I can call `smProviderModule.ioCacheDrop(repoInfo.owner, repoInfo.repo, 'openPrs', null)` guarded. That's the correct self-heal: drop the module-level cache entry, not a provider method.

BUT the tree must stay frozen during the full-suite run! I already started it. Per the pack's B6: files changed while a verification run is in flight → the run is stale; let it finish, then rerun. This fix is critical for correctness (self-heal would throw/do nothing). Make the edit NOW, let the current run finish (it validates everything else), then run a fresh full suite. Actually better: make the edit now, kill the in-flight run? B6 says do NOT kill mid-flight; let it finish, treat as stale, start fresh. But it takes ~3.5 min... Alternatively let it run (it still gives regression signal for the rest), apply the fix, then rerun full suite once. Accept the extra cycle.

